1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
Jay:

2
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.008
It is good to be here this morning with you guys. Excited to share. Thank you for the privilege.

3
00:00:07.008 --> 00:00:19.619
As Nate said, I am former pastor here at Hill Country Bible Church and then joined the staff of Mission of Hope Haiti a couple years ago and just excited to share this morning about God's design.

4
00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:25.957
In 1995, I graduated from Texas A&M and went to work with my parents in East Texas on Lake Conroe.

5
00:00:25.957 --> 00:00:39.732
They had started a marina back in the late 80s, and so the plan was for me to go to work with them and kind of help take over the business and let them retire and live happily ever after and all those good things.

6
00:00:39.732 --> 00:00:50.597
And as I was there, I joined my first church in the Woodlands called Faith Bible Church. And I began to volunteer there at Faith Bible Church and work at the same time.

7
00:00:50.597 --> 00:00:55.318
And the truth be known, the marina business did not demand a lot of time.

8
00:00:55.318 --> 00:01:06.118
And through the two and a half years that I was there, there was plenty of discretionary time that I had to invest in volunteering in the church that I was serving at.

9
00:01:06.118 --> 00:01:20.799
And so, through that process, a couple of guys started asking me questions and they started pressing in saying, "Hey, have you ever considered full-time ministry?" And all along my plan was I was going to graduate from college.

10
00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:33.087
I was going to go to work with my family. I was going to make a lot of money. And then I was going to invest that money in a Christian camp somewhere in East Texas. I loved camping ministry.

11
00:01:33.087 --> 00:01:41.559
Camping had such an impact on me. I had been to several different camps, Young Life Camp, and Pine Cove Christian Camps over there.

12
00:01:41.559 --> 00:01:50.419
And so it had such an impact on me I thought, man this would be a great way for me to contribute and give back. And so that was the plan.

13
00:01:50.419 --> 00:02:00.185
And some people that were in my life knew that and so they started to ask me, hey have you ever thought about going into full-time vocational ministry?

14
00:02:00.185 --> 00:02:09.821
And I said yeah I have thought about it but you know this is kind of the track I am on. And I kept getting this kind of just rubbing against my soul.

15
00:02:09.821 --> 00:02:21.980
And one day I decided, okay God here is the deal. If you want me in full-time ministry then you are going to have to blow open the doors because I am not going to go out and look for this.

16
00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:31.163
And this is just between you and me. So I did not have that conversation with my future wife. I did not have that conversation with my pastor.

17
00:02:31.163 --> 00:02:39.376
I did not tell anybody about that prayer because I was afraid if I shared that prayer with anybody that was going to come true.

18
00:02:39.376 --> 00:02:55.674
Well, four months later, the senior pastor at my church showed up and we had done some work on his boat and we went out on the lake and it was there on that lake that God had me with this Peter moment of are you going to step out in faith and trust me?

19
00:02:55.674 --> 00:03:19.345
And so he said, "Hey, Jay, we want you to consider coming on staff as our associate youth pastor and by the way, we will put you through Dallas Seminary and give you an education and then you work for us for 5 years and then after that, if you want to go do the camping thing, it is all you." And so it was that moment that I said, okay, God has answered the prayer.

20
00:03:19.345 --> 00:03:28.852
And it was crazy because literally I had told none of the closest people, nobody, and yet he had made it happen. And so I began to wrestle it down.

21
00:03:28.852 --> 00:03:39.264
It took me a couple of weeks to, even though I knew that the answer was going to be yes, it still took me a couple of weeks to pray through it and figure it out.

22
00:03:39.264 --> 00:03:46.960
So it was at that time then I joined Faith Bible Church's staff in 1998 and my life started to take a different change.

23
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:56.337
Instead of being bored and wondering where my significance was, I began to attach myself to the way that God had really wired me and designed me.

24
00:03:56.337 --> 00:04:05.197
And so I had that opportunity and it set my path along vocational full-time ministry and it has been a blast and it has been a great run.

25
00:04:05.197 --> 00:04:18.455
And this morning we are talking about what does it look like? What is God's design for us as men? Steve Jobs says this. He says design is not just what it looks like and feels like. Design is how it works.

26
00:04:18.455 --> 00:04:28.932
And so today, hopefully you can figure out a little bit more about how you work and God's design for you and the different context that that is going to play out.

27
00:04:28.932 --> 00:04:34.300
And my hope is that this morning you will get that a little bit more crystal clear.

28
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:45.230
So we are going to, if you will open your Bibles to Ephesians 2, chapter 10, we are going to start with just this general idea of design and what God's design for us is.

29
00:04:45.230 --> 00:05:02.498
Ephesians 2:10, a very familiar verse, says this, "For we are God's workmanship created in Christ Jesus to do good works which God prepared in advance for us to do." It is a really rich verse and there are three main things that I want to really pull from that verse.

30
00:05:02.498 --> 00:05:15.885
The first one is this: ownership. God is our daddy, right? We are God's workmanship. He has ownership of our lives. God of the universe created us in his image. Bestowed that privilege on us as his creation.

31
00:05:15.885 --> 00:05:29.984
Not on any other creation did he give that privilege. But not only that, he sent his son Jesus to buy us. He purchased our lives when he died on the cross for us. And so God is the owner. He has the rights to our life.

32
00:05:29.984 --> 00:05:43.501
The second piece is this: workmanship. He says that we are God's workmanship. That Greek word there is poiema, which is where we get the English word poem. I am a little bit disappointed that that is the word.

33
00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:54.625
To me, poems are just not that masculine. I do not get all this when I hear that. But the reality of being God's poetry is this: that there are no two poems that are alike.

34
00:05:54.625 --> 00:06:07.560
And so what it speaks to me and what I grab from that is that God has a unique design for us personally, not only generally but personally for how we are going to play into his grand scheme of things.

35
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.147
And so we are God's poetry, if you will.

36
00:06:10.147 --> 00:06:19.589
And then thirdly, it gives us purpose and design. So he has created these that we are his workmanship and he has prepared good works for us to do.

37
00:06:19.589 --> 00:06:31.942
He has got things for us to do and a significant role for us to play in his plan for creation. What I like to see is this is that we are saved to something and not necessarily from something.

38
00:06:31.942 --> 00:06:45.847
We talk about holiness, right? And there is this idea of holiness is about avoiding sin and cutting out sin in your life. And while that is an aspect of holiness, I think there is a much greater part about holiness.

39
00:06:45.847 --> 00:06:56.906
And holiness is this: that it means to be set apart. And I do not think it is just simply that we are set apart from sin. I think it is that we are set apart to something.

40
00:06:56.906 --> 00:07:09.776
And we are set apart to those good works that God has prepared beforehand for us to do. So God has saved us not just from something, but to something. He has got that significant role for us to play.

41
00:07:09.776 --> 00:07:22.775
So let us see how design plays out in other areas and aspects of our life. We are going to start with a man's design and marriage. A man's design in marriage. The first there is this: a natural leader.

42
00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:35.580
Natural leader types in marriage tend to be too assertive. We talk about the natural leader and we talk about that tendency. Of course this tendency is how the natural leader plays out in his flesh.

43
00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:49.291
He becomes too assertive. Another way of describing that is that he is a control freak. He likes to make the call, likes to make the decisions. He is the one that when it comes down to it, he will make that call.

44
00:07:49.291 --> 00:07:53.624
He will do whatever it takes. They are the take-charge kind of guy.

45
00:07:53.624 --> 00:08:06.623
How many people watch the championship game on Monday night, the NCAA basketball championship game? Was that not an unbelievable game? It was like one of the greatest basketball games I have ever seen.

46
00:08:06.623 --> 00:08:19.816
And I am thinking through those last 4.7 seconds, and they are huddled up as teams and they are talking through strategy and everything. And let me ask you a question. The guy that makes the shot for UNC.

47
00:08:19.816 --> 00:08:31.845
When it came down to who was going to get the ball and who was going to take the shot, you think he shied away from it? I think he was like, give me the rock. I want to shoot that thing.

48
00:08:31.845 --> 00:08:41.934
And I do not know if any of you have read or heard about kind of how he made that miracle shot. It was the ugliest looking shot I have ever seen in my life.

49
00:08:41.934 --> 00:08:55.127
He is driving down, he gets to the three-point line and he sees a guy wide open in the middle of the lane, and he is about to pass it and then he goes, no, wait. I got to shoot this. We are down by three.

50
00:08:55.127 --> 00:09:06.833
And he takes the three and somehow does this double clutch ridiculous shot and makes it. I mean, it was like, you got to be kidding me. This is like a magical Michael Jordan moment.

51
00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:21.061
Something that should not be done just happened. And that guy right there, I guarantee he is in the huddle. He is like, man, give me the rock. I am going to make sure that we do something. We are going to make it happen.

52
00:09:21.061 --> 00:09:22.807
He is going to take charge.

53
00:09:22.807 --> 00:09:34.577
Well, timeout. We are going to huddle up and Villanova is like, we are going to get our game plan now. And I am sitting there going, this is awesome. We are going into overtime here.

54
00:09:34.577 --> 00:09:48.482
We are going to have some more basketball to watch tonight. It is not over yet. And they are sitting there and they are talking and the guy that takes the shot for Villanova, you think he was ready to get that shot?

55
00:09:48.482 --> 00:10:02.063
You think he shied away from it? I do not think so. I think he was like, give me the rock. I want to shoot this thing. And you watch him. They go down and the guy sets the pick. The other guy follows up behind.

56
00:10:02.063 --> 00:10:13.122
He gets the pass. He shoots the thing. And everybody is going, no way. This is not happening. And just nails it. And the place goes crazy. That is the natural-born leader.

57
00:10:13.122 --> 00:10:21.077
That is the natural leader that says, hey, at the last second, give me the rock. I want to be the one that takes that shot.

58
00:10:21.077 --> 00:10:49.857
The problem is as we look at a verse in Colossians 3:19, it says this, "Husbands, love your wives and do not be harsh with them." "Children, obey your parents in everything, for this pleases the Lord." "Fathers, do not embitter your children or they will become discouraged." See, as those of us that are the natural leader, there is a tendency that we have in this control-freak realm, this assertive realm, that we start to demand a whole lot.

59
00:10:49.857 --> 00:11:00.463
And so, when the scripture says, love your wives and do not be harsh with them. See, the tendency that we can have is that we begin to demand too much of our wives.

60
00:11:00.463 --> 00:11:08.224
We begin to point and delegate and push and pull on them and ask them to do things and we become an overbearing husband.

61
00:11:08.224 --> 00:11:24.586
And that is the tendency that we can have and we have to really fight that and enter into what does it look like for us to be men and though we have been wired to be natural leaders, how we enter into that relationship with love and without being harsh.

62
00:11:24.586 --> 00:11:36.486
It is the same way for our kids. We as fathers can sometimes get too assertive. And it says, do not provoke your kids. That is a scripture that God gave me long before I ever had kids.

63
00:11:36.486 --> 00:12:01.321
And as a man that grew up in a home with parents that loved me greatly, my dad that loves me well and still does as a grown man today, there was still a part of the way that he disciplined me that was hard and was difficult and I was provoked at many times by his own anger towards me and obviously the effects of that sin affecting me as a man and having to really wrestle with that.

64
00:12:01.321 --> 00:12:10.763
Okay, what does it look like to provoke my kids to anger and how do I avoid doing that based on the way I am wired. So that is the natural leader.

65
00:12:10.763 --> 00:12:21.499
The second is the non-leader type. And the non-leader type in marriage tends to be too passive. They tend to let the wife lead and it becomes this very vicious cycle.

66
00:12:21.499 --> 00:12:28.031
The wife begins to be frustrated and the husband gets frustrated and then it is just this tough deal.

67
00:12:28.031 --> 00:12:44.846
So, I was counseling with a couple that I had known for a long time and kind of done life with them and the wife was just, she was at her wits' end and the husband was very resigned and just kind of doing his own thing, but he was always really short with her.

68
00:12:44.846 --> 00:12:55.582
He just had an edge about him that kept him from really treating her well with his words and just with the way his countenance was and the way that he approached her.

69
00:12:55.582 --> 00:13:08.969
And so we sat down, we started walking it through and I knew exactly what had been going on. See, his wife was the natural leader in the relationship and she had no problems taking over that leadership role.

70
00:13:08.969 --> 00:13:22.033
But at the true place of where she was, she still desired that her husband would lead. And so she would get frustrated and she would begin to nag him and press into him and just be that dripping faucet.

71
00:13:22.033 --> 00:13:35.938
But see, he had learned something in his way of doing this relationship. He had learned that when he goes out and he tries to lead and he does not do it right, he gets punished. He gets ridiculed. He gets belittled.

72
00:13:35.938 --> 00:13:47.062
And so the safe place for him was, I am just going to step away from that. I am going to let her do it because she is going to do it right. She knows how she wants it done.

73
00:13:47.062 --> 00:13:51.912
And the safer place is for me to resign to passivity and let her take over.

74
00:13:51.912 --> 00:14:04.135
And so I articulated this for them and the conversation was where I looked at the wife and I just said, hey, you are going to need to try something that is going to be really risky for you.

75
00:14:04.135 --> 00:14:16.035
And that is this: that you are going to have to give your husband permission to fail and then not punish him when he does, but love him through it. And to the husband, I looked at him.

76
00:14:16.035 --> 00:14:27.999
I said, listen, the reason why there is so much frustration is number one, she is still at the deepest place of who she is, she wants you to lead your family. She wants you to lead her.

77
00:14:27.999 --> 00:14:33.496
And so, you are going to have to take the risk and risk being hurt again as a leader.

78
00:14:33.496 --> 00:14:43.067
And you are going to have to start going back to the place where you once thought you might want to be and do it again and begin to lead more fully.

79
00:14:43.067 --> 00:14:56.002
And leadership in marriage is this. It is submitting your design to your biblical responsibilities rather than how you feel. See, he was dictated by his emotions. He did not feel like getting wounded.

80
00:14:56.002 --> 00:15:03.245
He did not feel like dealing with the junk that came with that. He did not feel like having to invite the fight.

81
00:15:03.245 --> 00:15:15.792
And honestly, feelings get to that place where they can really jack us up and keep us from making good decisions. And if we live underneath our feelings, we end up making a lot of bad decisions.

82
00:15:15.792 --> 00:15:26.463
See, I think feelings are this, guys. Feelings are like the dashboard of your car. God designed us with feelings to let us know what is going on underneath the hood.

83
00:15:26.463 --> 00:15:40.238
So when the anger rises up or the frustration rises up, the check engine light, that emotion that is there is the check engine light on our dashboard that says, hey, check out what is going on underneath the hood.

84
00:15:40.238 --> 00:15:54.207
You are about to have a breakdown. Or even the opposite. Maybe you are full of joy. Maybe you are full of excitement and happiness. Well, there is something inside underneath the hood that is making you run that way.

85
00:15:54.207 --> 00:16:03.908
But where we get into trouble is where we take what is on the dashboard and we do not take the look underneath the hood and we make radical decisions.

86
00:16:03.908 --> 00:16:16.390
So, as a guy that has been in the auto repair business, one of the reasons a check engine light will come on is because the gas cap is not turned on or it has not been cranked all the way down.

87
00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:30.230
And so it will send a signal to your car and then it will turn on the check engine light. And if you have had a wife that has done this, they kind of freak out going, oh my gosh, my check engine light is coming on.

88
00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:40.836
My car is about to blow up. What do I do? What do I do? Well, have you checked the gas cap? And when you tighten down the gas cap, the check engine light goes away.

89
00:16:40.836 --> 00:16:55.064
Well, imagine if you had said, hey, you know what, that means we need to go in and do a full tune-up, change out the spark plugs, clean the throttle body, get everything done and spend all this money and all unnecessary.

90
00:16:55.064 --> 00:17:04.700
It is an overreaction and that is our tendency that we can do if we are led by our emotions and we do not check what is going on underneath the hood.

91
00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:18.540
So, we need to accept those responsibilities. What are those responsibilities? What are the things that we need to submit our lives to? I propose that there are four things. The first is this. Follow Jesus closely.

92
00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:32.057
Follow Jesus closely. And I am not just saying, hey, go through the routine of reading your Bible regularly and setting up this legalistic system where we try to artificially create this relationship with God.

93
00:17:32.057 --> 00:17:43.116
I am talking about really enjoying God and allowing him to speak deeply into your souls. And of course, that involves reading your Bible. Of course, that involves praying.

94
00:17:43.116 --> 00:17:50.165
Of course, that involves a lot of the spiritual disciplines, getting that time away with him and in solitude.

95
00:17:50.165 --> 00:17:59.025
But it also includes just practicing his presence in your life every single day and being reminded of that and getting in tune with that.

96
00:17:59.025 --> 00:18:09.243
But there are also things that God gives us signals and he works on us and he knows how we are designed and there are certain things that he wants us to grab.

97
00:18:09.243 --> 00:18:20.302
So, a long time ago, when I was a younger guy, the Lord gave me several things that he wanted me to kind of tuck away in my soul and how he wanted me to function as a man.

98
00:18:20.302 --> 00:18:35.500
And so, I have got this daily reminder sheet of some significant verses and some things that God has just spoken into my soul that I keep with me in my Bible so that wherever I go, I look at these things and I will take myself to them.

99
00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:47.529
And some of them: he reminded me, Jay, gentleness is your strength. See as a strong man I have a tendency to take command and my intensity and my passion can sometimes be misinterpreted.

100
00:18:47.529 --> 00:18:58.717
And what I have to remember is that whatever intensity I think I am at, the people that are watching me or interacting with me, they think it is at another couple of levels.

101
00:18:58.717 --> 00:19:09.194
And God wanted to whisper into me and he just said, hey, gentleness is your strength. Dial it back a little bit. There is a time and a place for you to rev it up.

102
00:19:09.194 --> 00:19:20.059
Couple of other things he said. I am not going to read them all to you, but the one thing is nothing to prove and nothing to lose. Nothing to prove and nothing to lose.

103
00:19:20.059 --> 00:19:34.805
You know, as men, I think we fall into the temptation of trying to prove our significance either through our work or through getting the trophy wife or through having a significant position, whatever it is, that misplaced thing.

104
00:19:34.805 --> 00:19:45.411
We have got to remember that we have nothing to prove. God has already established our significance by dying on the cross. He paid the greatest price for our lives.

105
00:19:45.411 --> 00:19:58.734
There is no other greater price that can be paid for our lives. You cannot add to your significance. So, you got nothing to prove because God has already sealed the deal on that. He created us in his image.

106
00:19:58.734 --> 00:20:07.529
He died for us and he gave us a significant unique design to play a part in his eternal plan. How can you be more significant than that?

107
00:20:07.529 --> 00:20:20.852
No matter what you accomplish in your work, no matter what you accomplish in sports, no matter what you accomplish in school, no matter what happens in your family, you cannot be more significant than that.

108
00:20:20.852 --> 00:20:30.812
And if you begin to place your significance in those areas, it is going to destroy you because you are going to come up short because you are not perfect.

109
00:20:30.812 --> 00:20:38.637
And those things can never completely and consistently meet your need for significance. But if you look to Jesus, he can.

110
00:20:38.637 --> 00:20:52.736
Couple of other things. I have a deep-seated need for justice in my life. When things are not fair or when something goes wrong, I instantly have to, I like to be right. I am sure none of you struggle with that either.

111
00:20:52.736 --> 00:21:05.153
But when there is something that has gone wrong, I feel this thing that needs to be corrected and we need to right that wrong. And so, a verse that helps me with that is humility before honor.

112
00:21:05.153 --> 00:21:13.496
"By humility and the fear of the Lord are riches and honor and life." That is Proverbs 22:4. And so, the Lord just gives me that.

113
00:21:13.496 --> 00:21:19.898
So, on occasion, there are times, and especially as, Ryan, you probably get this as a youth pastor.

114
00:21:19.898 --> 00:21:31.345
There are times where when I was a young youth pastor, my students would go home and they would tell mom or dad or something about how something did not go right at youth group.

115
00:21:31.345 --> 00:21:38.459
And then mom or dad call me up and they just start eating on me and I would be like, you got to be kidding me.

116
00:21:38.459 --> 00:21:48.677
I may not be a ton older than your kid, but I am the grown adult here and you are going to take the word of your kid. And so I started to play this thing out.

117
00:21:48.677 --> 00:22:02.517
And the pastor that invested so much in my life, he just said, "Hey, Jay. Humility before honor. Just humility before honor. Do not worry about trying to prove that you are right. Just listen to them and love them.

118
00:22:02.517 --> 00:22:09.372
Shepherd them well. Speak the truth when it is necessary." You know, sometimes I just need to vent it out.

119
00:22:09.372 --> 00:22:20.625
And that was really good advice for me because I am not afraid of a good fight and that gets me into trouble a lot of times. So that first part there is follow Jesus closely.

120
00:22:20.625 --> 00:22:26.898
If we are really going to live out this design and understand it, we got to follow Jesus closely.

121
00:22:26.898 --> 00:22:37.569
The second thing is this. There are three other things that go right behind that. Provide, protect, and serve. Provide, protect, and serve. The first one is provide.

122
00:22:37.569 --> 00:22:49.857
You know, it is really easy for us to understand the first part of provision, and that is that we need to go make money. We got to support our family. We got to put our kids through college.

123
00:22:49.857 --> 00:23:01.692
We got to do all of those things. We have this pressure, this responsibility to make money. But there is this flip side of that, too. And the flip side of that is to control spending.

124
00:23:01.692 --> 00:23:07.448
So in my house, that is really directly speaking to me because my wife is the frugal one.

125
00:23:07.448 --> 00:23:18.507
I am the one that I can find the Benelli shotgun that needs to be in my house yesterday, or the new baby Glock single stack that I need to have, I need a new carry weapon.

126
00:23:18.507 --> 00:23:29.437
And she is like, yeah, you go ahead and get that gun. Let us find out what happens. But we all have our little hobbies, and we have our little toys that we need to have.

127
00:23:29.437 --> 00:23:37.327
But there is a part of us that some of us have been successful and some of us live very generously and we give a lot away.

128
00:23:37.327 --> 00:23:49.097
But I have also been challenged in that that it is not only about what you give away, it is also about what you consume. So, think about that as a man and as a leader in your family.

129
00:23:49.097 --> 00:23:57.957
The two things are not only to manage how much money you are making, but also to manage your expenses and what is going on with spending.

130
00:23:57.957 --> 00:24:09.275
The second part of that, or the third part, is protect. We are called to protect our wives and protect our kids. God ultimately has the responsibility to protect our families.

131
00:24:09.275 --> 00:24:22.986
At the end of the day, we cannot protect them completely like God does. At the end of the day, we do not love them as much as God does, but God has still put us in a role and in a position for us to protect them.

132
00:24:22.986 --> 00:24:34.498
And protection is not necessarily just from physical harm. While it includes that, there are a lot of other things. And so, I find myself having these conversations with my wife.

133
00:24:34.498 --> 00:24:39.930
The first and foremost is not just the physical thing, but the spiritual protection.

134
00:24:39.930 --> 00:24:49.954
There is a whole other world that we do not see, the spiritual realm that is going on that is going after the heart and soul of your wife and of your kids.

135
00:24:49.954 --> 00:25:00.172
And so we have to do our part in walking through that and looking for that and looking for the signs and protecting them from that enemy the best that we can.

136
00:25:00.172 --> 00:25:12.848
The other part is protecting our wives from themselves. There is an aspect of them. I have this conversation with my wife where she has a really strong schedule and it is she has got it built out.

137
00:25:12.848 --> 00:25:26.623
I mean, she is running every bit as hard as me. And my wife is a great shepherd, caregiver, loves people, and she gets asked to do a lot of good things. And she will come home and say, hey, I got this opportunity.

138
00:25:26.623 --> 00:25:38.393
I am like, hey, let us talk about this. What does that look like for you in your schedule? And when you start to say yes to that, it is going to add added stress and pressure to you.

139
00:25:38.393 --> 00:25:50.293
And is that really what God wants you to be doing? And so we process it. I do not make that decision for her. I let her do it. She is a big girl. She knows how to make those decisions.

140
00:25:50.293 --> 00:25:53.009
But we are going to process that together.

141
00:25:53.009 --> 00:26:06.784
And I think part of that is me helping protect her from overextending herself and getting frustrated and burned out and not being able to do her first ministry well, which is to be a wife and mother in our family.

142
00:26:06.784 --> 00:26:19.525
The other part of that is helping them with their emotions. Our wives being emotional beings, sometimes they let their emotions get the best of them, and we have got to help them walk through that.

143
00:26:19.525 --> 00:26:33.753
I have two daughters, a 12-year-old and 8-year-old. That 12-year-old is growing up too fast. As is the 8-year-old, but the 12-year-old getting close to that teenage time, there is a lot more of this going on in the home.

144
00:26:33.753 --> 00:26:46.882
Not a ton, but every man I have talked to that has had daughters says, listen, brace yourself because this is going to keep going till they get to college. So, a lot of times I find myself being referee.

145
00:26:46.882 --> 00:27:00.657
Like they get heated and it gets elevated up here and I am like, oh man, this is too much. Okay, you need to go to your room. You need to go to your room. Let us go have a little timeout. Let us talk this through.

146
00:27:00.657 --> 00:27:14.174
But there is a part of that that is real. And God needs us to step in and lead those things so that emotions both on my wife's side and on my daughter's side do not get in such a place that it gets them cross.

147
00:27:14.174 --> 00:27:18.701
And so I need to help lead through that. That is a part of protecting.

148
00:27:18.701 --> 00:27:29.049
The fourth one is this. Serve. We are called as men and our biblical responsibilities to serve our wives. Love our wives. And we love our wives through service.

149
00:27:29.049 --> 00:27:39.332
Whether that is praying with them, whether that is supporting them around the house or in other areas, supporting them with the kids, whatever that looks like.

150
00:27:39.332 --> 00:27:48.321
But I think ultimately our job in service is to set them up to live out their design as wife, as mother, as minister of the gospel as well.

151
00:27:48.321 --> 00:27:57.698
So, those are kind of the four things. Any man that taps into his biblical identity as a man and a leader can lead regardless of his personality.

152
00:27:57.698 --> 00:28:09.145
Some of you might be wrestling with this idea of, man, I am just not a natural leader. And what I would tell you is that you do not have to be a natural leader in order to lead.

153
00:28:09.145 --> 00:28:22.662
That God at the second that you placed your faith in him for the forgiveness of your sins changed you as a man. You are a new creation and he deposited everything that you need for life and godliness as a man.

154
00:28:22.662 --> 00:28:34.821
The spiritual and the physical have now lined up inside of you and you have the capacity to lead. But again, that takes walking closely with the Lord and figuring out what that looks like.

155
00:28:34.821 --> 00:28:46.527
But here is the thing, men are not victims. You are not a victim of your personality. You think you are wired one way, but the scriptures say that you have been wired the other way.

156
00:28:46.527 --> 00:28:49.890
You have been wired according to God and his design.

157
00:28:49.890 --> 00:29:02.889
All right. So, there are two marriage designs. You have a couple of circles on your sheet there. I forgot to ask for a whiteboard this morning. So I am going to have to try to help you figure this out.

158
00:29:02.889 --> 00:29:12.460
But on your handout there, at number one, you have the top compliant wife. And then at the top of that on the right you have natural leader husband.

159
00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:26.106
So we are going to talk a little bit about kind of how those things intersect each other. On the compliant wife side, the natural leader husband will normally tend to marry a compliant wife. It is just the norm.

160
00:29:26.106 --> 00:29:39.493
It is not good bad or ugly or the other. It is just that is just what normally happens. And in that relationship what happens is on the left hand side under compliant wife they become an enabler if you will.

161
00:29:39.493 --> 00:29:53.074
So in that little box there, enabler. And then on the right hand side the natural leader husband can become a tyrant. So in that little box there, tyrant. He is that control freak. He takes that assertive role.

162
00:29:53.074 --> 00:30:06.332
He starts to demand that stuff. And then in the middle when those things intersect because she is compliant and because he is ultra assertive in the middle you end up with one idea. Guess whose idea it is?

163
00:30:06.332 --> 00:30:19.655
It is the husband's idea. And so the thing that happens in that little exchange and in that marriage is at some point the wife becomes irrelevant. Her opinion, her thoughts, her ideas do not matter anymore.

164
00:30:19.655 --> 00:30:36.535
And so we as Christian men and as leaders that maybe are in that natural leader category need to be able to walk through that and we need to start having conversations of inviting our wives in to the conversation, helping them, or asking them to help govern us.

165
00:30:36.535 --> 00:30:48.888
They act as a governor. We start going too fast, they can ask questions and help us process so that we do not become that tyrant and we do not allow that whole process to make her irrelevant.

166
00:30:48.888 --> 00:30:55.808
So over time in that model she will feel more marginalized and at the end of the day that does not go well.

167
00:30:55.808 --> 00:31:03.827
At the end of the day it ends in resignation on her part and a husband that is frustrated and wondering where his wife went.

168
00:31:03.827 --> 00:31:13.528
Number two there you have what happens when you have natural leader wife on the left and then compliant husband on the right. The natural leader wife.

169
00:31:13.528 --> 00:31:20.836
She is looking for her husband to act and he is not acting and so she becomes the nag in that box there, the nag.

170
00:31:20.836 --> 00:31:34.676
And then on the right hand side the husband learns, hey, it is just better just not to go against the grain and let her lead and just kind of passively pull out. And he becomes the whipped puppy. The whipped puppy.

171
00:31:34.676 --> 00:31:45.476
So that thing that really irritated you when you were in junior high or high school and people used to say, man, you are whipped by that girl. Right now it is reality.

172
00:31:45.476 --> 00:31:57.699
And so when you have these two personalities going and married together and in union, there is an important thing that needs to happen. And that is there needs to be a bridge that is built.

173
00:31:57.699 --> 00:32:08.693
And that bridge can be built as you take responsibility for those four things that we talked about. Following Jesus closely, providing, protecting, and serving your wife.

174
00:32:08.693 --> 00:32:14.190
And as you live those things out, it will overcome the dynamics of that relationship.

175
00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:27.448
Let us talk about a man's design in his work. Starting with design and career, a match is critical. Lining up who you are with your career is absolutely essential. So, I shared a little bit about my story.

176
00:32:27.448 --> 00:32:35.597
I am going to share a little bit more about it. So, I ended up going to Faith Bible Church. I was on staff there for 10 years.

177
00:32:35.597 --> 00:32:49.178
In the ninth year, I began to get restless and I had gone from associate youth pastor to youth pastor to adult ministries. And through that time grew up as a man and as a minister of the gospel and as a pastor.

178
00:32:49.178 --> 00:32:57.909
And about year nine I started to get restless thinking okay it is time to make a change it is just time for me to get a new assignment.

179
00:32:57.909 --> 00:33:09.421
And so I began to prepare a resume and start walking through that and something became very clear to me as I began to prepare my resume. My first job I was working for my family.

180
00:33:09.421 --> 00:33:21.580
That does not really bode well when you have had two jobs and one of them was working for your family when you are going out and looking for a job. There is some stuff that goes with that.

181
00:33:21.580 --> 00:33:34.903
And then the second part was everything that I had done in one spot for the last 10 years or nine years. And so that was fine. I was like, okay, I can wrestle that down. I got this and I am a good salesman.

182
00:33:34.903 --> 00:33:48.161
I can kind of figure that all out. But the other part of it was I did not know who I was. I had experienced success in ministry. God had used me to help build a youth ministry, very healthy youth ministry.

183
00:33:48.161 --> 00:33:59.867
God had helped me to build a very healthy small group ministry in our church and had given me influence and those sorts of things. But I did not know why or how that had come about.

184
00:33:59.867 --> 00:34:11.831
And so I finally took a test and it was not Myers-Briggs and it was not the DISC test because most people already knew what I was. They could just tell me without me taking those tests.

185
00:34:11.831 --> 00:34:24.636
But it was this test called StrengthsFinder. How many people have taken StrengthsFinder? Awesome. I do not know about you, but when I took that test, you can buy the test for like 20 bucks. Take it.

186
00:34:24.636 --> 00:34:38.541
It will take you about 30 minutes online. But I felt like, okay, I really need to understand myself a little bit better so I can understand the role for me in the next assignment that I get. And so I took that test.

187
00:34:38.541 --> 00:34:45.914
After I took that test, I was like, this is unbelievable. This thing nailed me to a T. So I took the profile home.

188
00:34:45.914 --> 00:34:59.431
I did not tell my wife anything and I put it on the breakfast table and let her read it and I just said, hey, tell me what you think about it. She goes, well, this is you. I am like, I know. Is that not crazy?

189
00:34:59.431 --> 00:35:06.868
This test had helped me understand how I was wired and what I was good at and the lane that I should be running in.

190
00:35:06.868 --> 00:35:16.310
And so I grabbed that and I knew exactly then as I was praying and walking through what the next assignment was going to be, where I needed to go.

191
00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:27.434
So I ended up going over to Naples, Florida and eventually became an executive pastor at a church there in Naples, Florida and was serving there for three and a half years.

192
00:35:27.434 --> 00:35:41.209
That is how I got connected with Mission of Hope was during the earthquake. I was there and we were one of the anchor churches back then along with Hill Country. And so that was also my connection to Hill Country.

193
00:35:41.209 --> 00:35:47.935
I met Bob King and then this led to that and ended up coming on staff here at Hill Country Bible Church.

194
00:35:47.935 --> 00:35:58.735
And then later as I was on staff here, I just started to get another wrestle and the wrestle was man, maybe I need to do something different. And I will tell you this.

195
00:35:58.735 --> 00:36:10.117
When you start to have these wrestles, do not pull the trigger too quick. Do not pull the trigger too quickly. I pulled the trigger too quickly. I left and I got into business.

196
00:36:10.117 --> 00:36:16.843
And I thought, you know, my dad was a businessman. I have got the wiring. I can go and be a businessman.

197
00:36:16.843 --> 00:36:27.320
And the fact is I believe I can still be a good businessman today, even though business has not really gone that well for me. But I feel like I am wired for that.

198
00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:44.070
But there is a part of me that believes that God is thwarting that, that he thwarted that because it goes back to that original thing where I was when I prayed, God, if you want me in full-time vocational ministry, then you are going to have to open the door.

199
00:36:44.070 --> 00:36:57.134
And he clearly did that and I walked through that door and then I tried to walk out. But God had designed me for a different role on this earth. And as much as I wanted to fight against it, I could not.

200
00:36:57.134 --> 00:37:11.297
And so I had the opportunity then to join Mission of Hope staff. And I can tell you this that that got me back in line with how God had wired me. And now I am in a spot at age 43 where my design matches up with my role.

201
00:37:11.297 --> 00:37:16.082
And there is no greater fulfillment that a man can have when that happens.

202
00:37:16.082 --> 00:37:22.549
Now there are some of you here who are like, man, I do not know if that will ever happen in my life.

203
00:37:22.549 --> 00:37:30.504
And I was speaking with the number two guy from ExxonMobil not too long ago and he said, Jay, very few men love their jobs.

204
00:37:30.504 --> 00:37:44.344
And that is, it really breaks my heart as I say that because I love my job and I love my job I think because it is so closely associated to how I am wired. The role and how I am wired are just meant for each other.

205
00:37:44.344 --> 00:37:54.627
And so there still is hope. It is never too late. Process that. Invite other men into that process with you if you are looking for something along those lines.

206
00:37:54.627 --> 00:38:06.333
But let us talk about the career transitions and the search for a match. In your early 20s. It is the have-to time. You get out of college, you are trying to figure out who you are.

207
00:38:06.333 --> 00:38:15.840
You know that you have to go get a job. Now you have spent all this money on an education. It is time you got to go get a job. You got to get paid.

208
00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:28.710
You got to get off the parents' payroll, get on your own payroll, and figure it out and figure out what the best fit is. A lot of times during our 20s, we are just trying a bunch of different things.

209
00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:35.177
And so we might have a couple of different jobs trying to figure it out and see what God has for us.

210
00:38:35.177 --> 00:38:44.748
Then you move into the critical 30s. This is the need-to time. You are growing up, you are becoming an adult, you are probably starting your family.

211
00:38:44.748 --> 00:38:57.489
And it is the time where you need to go ahead and figure out who you are. And it is the time where you also start to gain more convictions. You start to gain a better sense of your personal values.

212
00:38:57.489 --> 00:39:01.886
You start to gain more experiences. You start to grab more passions.

213
00:39:01.886 --> 00:39:12.428
And so in that time, you start to understand a little bit more about who you are and how you are wired and what the better fit for you is from a career standpoint.

214
00:39:12.428 --> 00:39:24.457
Then you move into the midlife 40s. This is must-do time, because your kids are growing up. They are about to hit college and all that stuff. I am 43. I got a 12-year-old and 8-year-old.

215
00:39:24.457 --> 00:39:38.426
I am looking at six years down the road. I got a kid that is going to go to Texas A&M. They have no other choice. And I got to figure out how I am going to pay for it. So it is that time where it is the must-do time.

216
00:39:38.426 --> 00:39:51.749
It is where you have to figure out what your lane is and run in that lane. A lot of guys at this time, I was 40 years old when I made that decision. Think about that. When I pulled that trigger too quickly.

217
00:39:51.749 --> 00:40:05.460
I was 40 years old. A lot of guys when they get into this midlife 40s, they forget where their lane is and they start trying to drift. They drift over into other areas to say maybe the road is smoother over here.

218
00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:18.977
Maybe I can put it on cruise control over here and just kick back a little bit. If you make that lane change too quickly and it is not the right lane, you could find yourself on a gravel road doing a tailspin.

219
00:40:18.977 --> 00:40:30.295
And when you get into that other lane that is not your lane, you get immensely frustrated. And then what happens is you start to blame people. You start to blame other people.

220
00:40:30.295 --> 00:40:43.618
Well, I took this job because it made a lot of money and I needed to provide for our family, babe. And so that is the conversation that starts to be had. Becomes your wife's fault, becomes your kids' fault.

221
00:40:43.618 --> 00:40:55.971
Oh, you do not necessarily say it to your kids. You just say, man, I needed to go and pay for college and so I took this job because I had to make the money. And you start to do those things.

222
00:40:55.971 --> 00:41:05.348
And then what happens is because all of that just goes awry, then you start to decide, okay, well, maybe I can trade up. Maybe I need a new wife.

223
00:41:05.348 --> 00:41:15.308
Maybe I need to trade work for pleasure and I need to get that screaming Corvette or that center console boat. Or maybe I just need some more time for me.

224
00:41:15.308 --> 00:41:22.228
Those are the things that we can do that when we get out of our lane, we start to make really poor choices.

225
00:41:22.228 --> 00:41:30.441
And the reality is that as you look back and you see the cloud of dust that is behind you, you have left a path of destruction.

226
00:41:30.441 --> 00:41:33.998
Fourth one is the relaxed 50s. This is the can-do time.

227
00:41:33.998 --> 00:41:46.933
So a lot of guys they get into this part of their life and they have had their peak earning years, but they also realize that during this time it is an opportunity for them to invest back into others.

228
00:41:46.933 --> 00:41:59.609
And so at Mission of Hope, one of my jobs is I do development. And so I help raise funds for the mission. And so I get to sit with a lot of guys that are kind of in this life stage. It is a blast.

229
00:41:59.609 --> 00:42:13.384
It is a blast because I get to be a pastor to them and help shepherd them, not necessarily to send their money to Mission of Hope, but just to help them walk through what does that look like in that phase of life.

230
00:42:13.384 --> 00:42:18.558
Bob Buford wrote a book called *Halftime*. You have probably heard of that book.

231
00:42:18.558 --> 00:42:30.328
It is a great book about this whole cycle of about you spend the first half of your life earning things and getting to a spot and you kind of get to that spot and you go, is this it?

232
00:42:30.328 --> 00:42:45.720
And you get to that place, you go, well, maybe now it is a time for me to make a change and now I can start, all this stuff that I have accumulated and where I have gotten to, now it is an opportunity for me to invest it back into others.

233
00:42:45.720 --> 00:42:58.202
And so that is really that stage of life is learning how to then invest your life experience, your money and your time that you have to give away and start investing that into kingdom purposes.

234
00:42:58.202 --> 00:43:12.430
So there are two important questions for every man that he needs to answer as we walk through this. What do I do well and with satisfaction? What do I do well and with satisfaction? So the well part, it fits your design.

235
00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:25.559
What is the thing that God designed you to do? And then what is the thing that you are passionate about doing that lines up with that? So those two things have to sync up if we are going to find a match.

236
00:43:25.559 --> 00:43:32.737
I was meeting with one of our young adults here at Hill Country Bible Church and he was early on in his career.

237
00:43:32.737 --> 00:43:46.060
He had played baseball at a small college in South Texas and his dream was to always go into and to become a professional baseball player. Like none of us ever dreamed of being professional athletes, right?

238
00:43:46.060 --> 00:43:53.497
But that was one of his dreams and he was a really good baseball player, but it did not end up working out for him.

239
00:43:53.497 --> 00:44:01.258
So, he had to go out and get a job and he ended up going into kind of the wealth management financial planning industry.

240
00:44:01.258 --> 00:44:09.019
And as he was in that job, he worked there for a couple years and it was just a grind and he was not wired for that job.

241
00:44:09.019 --> 00:44:21.048
And at the end of the day was getting pressure from his employer to do things that he did not feel like were the right things to do and a lot of those things that go on in that scenario.

242
00:44:21.048 --> 00:44:33.465
And so he began to invite people to speak into his life. And one of the questions that I asked along with several other people is you love baseball. You are coaching little league on the side.

243
00:44:33.465 --> 00:44:47.176
Have you thought about being a coach? Well, no. I had not thought about that. Well, maybe you need to think about that. Took him a couple of months. He finally landed on a place of I am going to give this a shot.

244
00:44:47.176 --> 00:45:01.145
So, he ends up he joins a school in Round Rock ISD and becomes a coach, coaches football and baseball and loves it and he is doing great and he comes home happy at night. He comes home energized and not drained. Why?

245
00:45:01.145 --> 00:45:14.597
Because he is in his lane. And so for us, we got to figure out what are those things that we do well. He was a really good coach and then with satisfaction coaching matching up with baseball met his passions.

246
00:45:14.597 --> 00:45:17.636
The two married and it was a great fit for him.

247
00:45:17.636 --> 00:45:31.088
Second part of that is what are the things I want to do but cannot. What are the things that I want to do but cannot. The no is as important as the yes. When I was in my early 30s, I was in pretty good shape.

248
00:45:31.088 --> 00:45:44.411
I kind of hit that. I was a skinny kid growing up and through high school and then about 22 I kind of started filling out and my body started catching up with what my mind thought I should be as an athlete.

249
00:45:44.411 --> 00:45:58.445
And so I began to really develop and I was playing a lot of sports and doing a lot of stuff and then one of my friends one day, I loved football, grew up playing football, played for 11 years, did not play in college.

250
00:45:58.445 --> 00:46:12.091
Was not good enough to do that, but I still had this idea that I could still play. It is hilarious. So, one of my buddies comes, hey, did you know there is a minor league football team here in Montgomery County?

251
00:46:12.091 --> 00:46:26.254
I said, what? He said, yeah, there is a minor league football team here in Montgomery County. Put on the pads, full contact, minor league football team. He is like, yeah. I am like, oh, I am in. We are going to do this.

252
00:46:26.254 --> 00:46:38.413
I am 30 years old. I am 30 years old. I am going to go back and play football. I had not played in 12 years, but I am going to go back and play. Stupid, stupid. I am going to break a knee.

253
00:46:38.413 --> 00:46:51.865
I am going to be crippled for the rest of my life. I am going to get a concussion. All that stuff. It was just dumb. But that was a thing that I wanted to do but I could not do. I never went and tried it out.

254
00:46:51.865 --> 00:46:55.874
I was smart enough not to do that. So not all Aggies are dumb.

255
00:46:55.874 --> 00:47:02.018
Steve Jobs says this. He is talking about branding and kind of design in the midst of branding.

256
00:47:02.018 --> 00:47:10.555
He says this, "And it comes from saying no to a thousand things to make sure we do not get on the wrong track or try to do too much.

257
00:47:10.555 --> 00:47:27.305
We are always thinking about new markets we could enter, but it is only by saying no that you can concentrate on the things that are really important." So guys, while it is really healthy to dream, make sure those dreams are within a certain realm of reality.

258
00:47:27.305 --> 00:47:32.414
That the things of what you want to do line up with the things that you can do.

259
00:47:32.414 --> 00:47:45.155
Say no to some of those things that you are just sitting there going, that would be really cool, and I am envious of that, but I got to move on. I got to let it go and allow that freedom to set in.

260
00:47:45.155 --> 00:47:52.980
Couple of other things real quick. These two questions, I want to just say this. You cannot answer these questions alone.

261
00:47:52.980 --> 00:48:07.079
Do not answer these questions without the closest men in your life speaking into them and you inviting them to give you feedback. So, when I made that quick trigger pull, I am, you can kind of tell I am an intense guy.

262
00:48:07.079 --> 00:48:17.362
I am real aggressive. I kind of get my mind made up and I am going after it. I do not let a lot of stuff get in my way and when it does, I kind of run it over.

263
00:48:17.362 --> 00:48:30.232
And when I was going through that time, I talked to a couple people that have permission to speak into my life that I have said, I am giving you permission to speak into my life. Talk to me. Give it.

264
00:48:30.232 --> 00:48:42.067
I was so strong in my assertion of what I was going to do next that I muted them. I muted them. So, the way that I presented what I was going to do next did not invite a conversation.

265
00:48:42.067 --> 00:48:55.972
It did not invite them to say, hey, have you thought about this? I muted them and just bulldozed ahead. And I will tell you, there was a time where there was some unrest and unpeace in my life and it was a struggle.

266
00:48:55.972 --> 00:49:02.439
I am thankful that God took me off that path and saved me from that. But that was a big deal for me.

267
00:49:02.439 --> 00:49:14.986
And so remember when you get into that place and you are trying to find that match or you are thinking about a career change, invite the men in your life to speak into you to tell you the truth.

268
00:49:14.986 --> 00:49:22.941
Whatever you do, the scripture says, "Whatever you do, work at it with all your heart as working for the Lord, not for men.

269
00:49:22.941 --> 00:49:30.572
Since you know that you will receive an inheritance from the Lord as a reward, it is the Lord Christ you are serving."

270
00:49:30.572 --> 00:49:42.795
We are about out of time here. I am going to run through the last section of our notes here. We are going to run through this pretty quick. Part four there. A man's design and his ministry.

271
00:49:42.795 --> 00:49:53.337
Every man has been gifted for ministry. Every man has been gifted for ministry. Romans 12:6 through 8. "We have different gifts according to the grace given to us.

272
00:49:53.337 --> 00:50:05.107
If a man's gift is prophesying, let him use it in proportion to his faith. If it is serving, let him serve. If it is teaching, let him teach. If it is encouraging, let him encourage.

273
00:50:05.107 --> 00:50:12.738
If it is contributing to the needs of others, let him give generously. If it is leadership, let him govern diligently.

274
00:50:12.738 --> 00:50:24.702
If it is showing mercy, let him do it cheerfully." When you become a believer in Jesus Christ, the Holy Spirit gives you a gift. That gift may differ throughout your lifespan, whatever.

275
00:50:24.702 --> 00:50:35.438
We do not know a ton about spiritual gifts and how God chooses to bestow those on us. But the reality is that every believer is equipped with a gift, is given a gift.

276
00:50:35.438 --> 00:50:48.502
And if the Holy Spirit, if God gives you a gift, then you probably have a responsibility with that. You need to use that. Those gifts fall into really two categories. A speaking gift and a serving gift.

277
00:50:48.502 --> 00:50:58.462
Letter C there. Using your gifts is one of the most important factors in experiencing God and finding real life. So your career is not going to be enough.

278
00:50:58.462 --> 00:51:07.904
Your family, investing all your time internally in your family is not going to be enough until you tie yourself to something that is transcendent.

279
00:51:07.904 --> 00:51:17.087
Until you tie yourself into something that requires God's activity to be able to accomplish it, you are going to always have that frustration.

280
00:51:17.087 --> 00:51:29.375
You are going to find real life when you tie yourself into that transcendent thing. That is why I came to Hill Country Bible Church because we are about reaching every man, woman, and child.

281
00:51:29.375 --> 00:51:43.280
That is a transcendent vision that has been given to this church. And that transcendent vision is something that ultimately if we are going to be successful, it is going to require that God intervenes on our behalf.

282
00:51:43.280 --> 00:51:55.827
If we are going to change Austin, if we are going to change Texas, if we are going to change the US, if we are going to change the world, it is going to require that God enters in on our behalf.

283
00:51:55.827 --> 00:52:09.020
Tie your life to something that is transcendent. There are a few verses there behind that on your, in your time today as you reflect back on this morning, check those things out. There is part four there.

284
00:52:09.020 --> 00:52:19.303
Some of y'all may have missed on the gifts. Number one is speaking. Number two is serving. Just for those that need to make sure all your blanks are filled in.

285
00:52:19.303 --> 00:52:32.949
Other areas of design. You were designed for relationships. You were made in the image of God. God exists in trinitarian community. He exists in relationship with the Father and with the Son and the Holy Spirit.

286
00:52:32.949 --> 00:52:41.680
He created you in the image. He created you to live in relationship. The first thing he said that was not good was for man to be alone.

287
00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:55.779
Scripture after scripture talks about how it is important for man to do life together. Iron sharpens iron. You were designed for relationships. Your tendency, my tendency is to isolate. Especially when things go wrong.

288
00:52:55.779 --> 00:53:09.878
And when things get tough, we tend to isolate. Why? Because relationships are hard and they are messy and we do not really know how to navigate it all. We need to enter into that awkwardness. Lean into the awkwardness.

289
00:53:09.878 --> 00:53:10.678
As Tim says.

290
00:53:10.678 --> 00:53:13.135
Letter B, you were designed for truth.

291
00:53:13.135 --> 00:53:27.881
Colossians 3:16 says, "Let the word dwell in you richly." "You will know the truth and the truth will set you free." If you feel like you are in bondage, if you feel like you are being held back, you need to get into God's word.

292
00:53:27.881 --> 00:53:41.721
God's word is the thing that is going to set you free as a man because it is going to help you understand who you are and how God wired you and help you live that out. John 17:17 says, "Sanctify them by your truth.

293
00:53:41.721 --> 00:53:44.502
Your word is truth." Get in the scriptures.

294
00:53:44.502 --> 00:53:57.631
You were designed for worship. You become like what you worship. Christian hedonism, as Piper says, God is most glorified in us when we are most satisfied in him. Learn to worship God in all that you do.

295
00:53:57.631 --> 00:54:11.859
And lastly, you were designed for eternity. Ecclesiastes 3:11 says he set eternity in the hearts of men. You are going to always long for more. There is always going to be a longing for more because this is not our home.

296
00:54:11.859 --> 00:54:25.117
This is not our home. Our home is with him. This is our temporary place. And eventually we are going to be reunited with the King of Kings and the Lord of Lords. And it is going to be a glorious day. Amen.

297
00:54:25.117 --> 00:54:32.878
Guys, thanks for letting me share with you this morning. Let me close this in prayer and you guys can go to your groups.

298
00:54:32.878 --> 00:54:52.021
God, thanks that you care about us as men enough that you would give us a unique design for each of us and Lord that you would invite us into your grand plan that we do have significance and that significance is found solely in you and in your son Jesus and all that you have accomplished for us.

299
00:54:52.021 --> 00:55:01.010
And God, I thank you that we have nothing to lose and nothing to prove. So God, as men, may we go out and may we accept our responsibility.

300
00:55:01.010 --> 00:55:09.000
May we reject passivity and may we rest in the greater reward that you have for us. It is in the name of Jesus that we pray. Amen.
